## Business Logic Patterns

### 1. Validation and Business Rules
```java
@Service
public class TimesheetService {
    
    public TimesheetResponseDto createTimesheet(TimesheetRequestDto request) {
        // Validate business rules
        validateTimesheetCreation(request);
        
        // Check permissions
        checkCreatePermission(request.candidateId(), request.jobId());
        
        // Create timesheet
        Timesheet timesheet = timesheetMapper.toEntity(request);
        Timesheet savedTimesheet = timesheetRepository.save(timesheet);
        
        // Apply business rules
        applyTimesheetRules(savedTimesheet);
        
        return timesheetMapper.toResponseDto(savedTimesheet);
    }
    
    private void validateTimesheetCreation(TimesheetRequestDto request) {
        // Check if candidate exists and is active
        if (!candidateRepository.existsByIdAndStatus(request.candidateId(), "ACTIVE")) {
            throw new ValidationErrorException("Candidate is not active");
        }
        
        // Check if job exists and is active
        if (!jobRepository.existsByIdAndStatus(request.jobId(), "ACTIVE")) {
            throw new ValidationErrorException("Job is not active");
        }
        
        // Check for duplicate timesheet
        if (timesheetRepository.existsByCandidateIdAndJobIdAndDate(
                request.candidateId(), request.jobId(), request.date())) {
            throw new ValidationErrorException("Timesheet already exists for this date");
        }
    }
}
```

### 2. Permission Checking Integration
```java
@Service
public class TimesheetService {
    
    public TimesheetResponseDto getTimesheetById(Integer id) {
        // Check permission using bulk permission checker
        BulkPermissionCheckRequest request = BulkPermissionCheckRequest.builder()
            .items(List.of(BulkPermissionCheckRequest.BulkPermissionCheckItem.builder()
                .entity(Entity.TIMESHEET)
                .permissionCheckContext(PermissionCheckContext.builder()
                    .permission(Permission.VIEW_TIMESHEET)
                    .permissionLevel(PermissionLevel.YES)
                    .build())
                .accessControlCheckMetadataContext(AccessControlCheckMetadataContext.builder()
                    .timesheetId(id)
                    .build())
                .build()))
            .build();
        
        BulkPermissionCheckResult result = bulkPermissionChecker.checkBulkPermissions(request);
        
        if (!result.getResults().get(0).isAllowed()) {
            throw new UnauthorizedAccessException("Access denied to timesheet");
        }
        
        // Get timesheet
        Timesheet timesheet = timesheetRepository.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("Timesheet not found"));
        
        return timesheetMapper.toResponseDto(timesheet);
    }
}
```

### 3. Bulk Operations
```java
@Service
public class TimesheetService {
    
    @Transactional
    public BulkTimesheetResponseDto bulkCreateTimesheets(BulkTimesheetRequestDto request) {
        List<TimesheetResponseDto> successful = new ArrayList<>();
        List<BulkErrorDto> errors = new ArrayList<>();
        
        // Check permissions for all timesheets
        BulkPermissionCheckRequest permissionRequest = buildPermissionRequest(request.timesheets());
        BulkPermissionCheckResult permissionResult = bulkPermissionChecker.checkBulkPermissions(permissionRequest);
        
        // Process each timesheet
        for (int i = 0; i < request.timesheets().size(); i++) {
            TimesheetRequestDto timesheetRequest = request.timesheets().get(i);
            
            try {
                // Check individual permission
                if (!permissionResult.getResults().get(i).isAllowed()) {
                    errors.add(new BulkErrorDto(i, "Access denied", "PERMISSION_DENIED"));
                    continue;
                }
                
                // Validate and create timesheet
                Timesheet timesheet = createTimesheetEntity(timesheetRequest);
                Timesheet savedTimesheet = timesheetRepository.save(timesheet);
                successful.add(timesheetMapper.toResponseDto(savedTimesheet));
                
            } catch (Exception ex) {
                logger.error("Failed to create timesheet at index " + i, ex);
                errors.add(new BulkErrorDto(i, ex.getMessage(), "CREATION_FAILED"));
            }
        }
        
        return new BulkTimesheetResponseDto(successful, errors, 
            new BulkOperationSummary(successful.size(), errors.size()));
    }
}
```

## Transaction Management

### 1. Service-Level Transactions
```java
@Service
@Transactional
public class TimesheetService {
    
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public List<TimesheetResponseDto> getTimesheetsByCandidate(Integer candidateId) {
        // Read-only transaction for queries
        return timesheetRepository.findByCandidateId(candidateId)
            .stream()
            .map(timesheetMapper::toResponseDto)
            .toList();
    }
    
    @Transactional
    public TimesheetResponseDto updateTimesheet(Integer id, TimesheetRequestDto request) {
        // Write transaction for updates
        Timesheet timesheet = timesheetRepository.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("Timesheet not found"));
        
        // Update fields
        timesheet.setName(request.name());
        timesheet.setStatus(request.status());
        
        // Save and return
        Timesheet savedTimesheet = timesheetRepository.save(timesheet);
        return timesheetMapper.toResponseDto(savedTimesheet);
    }
}
```

### 2. Complex Transaction Scenarios
```java
@Service
public class TimesheetApprovalService {
    
    @Transactional
    public void approveTimesheet(Integer timesheetId, String approverId) {
        // Load timesheet with related data
        Timesheet timesheet = timesheetRepository.findByIdWithRelatedEntities(timesheetId)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("Timesheet not found"));
        
        // Validate approval rules
        validateApprovalRules(timesheet, approverId);
        
        // Update timesheet status
        timesheet.setStatus("APPROVED");
        timesheet.setApprovedBy(approverId);
        timesheet.setApprovedAt(LocalDateTime.now());
        
        // Create approval record
        TimesheetApproval approval = new TimesheetApproval();
        approval.setTimesheetId(timesheetId);
        approval.setApproverId(approverId);
        approval.setApprovedAt(LocalDateTime.now());
        timesheetApprovalRepository.save(approval);
        
        // Update related entities
        updateRelatedEntities(timesheet);
        
        // Send notifications
        notificationService.sendApprovalNotification(timesheet);
    }
}
```

## Error Handling

### 1. Service-Level Exception Handling
```java
@Service
public class TimesheetService {
    
    public TimesheetResponseDto createTimesheet(TimesheetRequestDto request) {
        try {
            // Validate input
            validateTimesheetRequest(request);
            
            // Check permissions
            checkPermissions(request);
            
            // Create timesheet
            Timesheet timesheet = timesheetMapper.toEntity(request);
            Timesheet savedTimesheet = timesheetRepository.save(timesheet);
            
            return timesheetMapper.toResponseDto(savedTimesheet);
            
        } catch (ValidationErrorException ex) {
            logger.warn("Validation error creating timesheet: " + ex.getMessage());
            throw ex;
        } catch (UnauthorizedAccessException ex) {
            logger.warn("Permission denied creating timesheet: " + ex.getMessage());
            throw ex;
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            logger.error("Unexpected error creating timesheet", ex);
            throw new ServiceException("Failed to create timesheet", ex);
        }
    }
}
```

### 2. Custom Service Exceptions
```java
public class ServiceException extends RuntimeException {
    private final String errorCode;
    
    public ServiceException(String message, Throwable cause) {
        super(message, cause);
        this.errorCode = "SERVICE_ERROR";
    }
    
    public ServiceException(String message, String errorCode) {
        super(message);
        this.errorCode = errorCode;
    }
}
```

## Business Rule Engine Integration

### 1. Rule Engine Service
```java
@Service
public class RuleEngineService {
    
    private final IRuleEngine ruleEngine;
    private final RuleEngineMapper ruleEngineMapper;
    
    public RuleValidationResult validateTimesheetRules(Timesheet timesheet) {
        // Convert timesheet to rule engine format
        RuleEngineInputDto input = ruleEngineMapper.toRuleEngineInput(timesheet);
        
        // Execute rules
        RuleEngineResult result = ruleEngine.evaluateRules(input);
        
        // Convert result
        return ruleEngineMapper.toValidationResult(result);
    }
    
    public void applyTimesheetRules(Timesheet timesheet) {
        RuleValidationResult validation = validateTimesheetRules(timesheet);
        
        if (!validation.isValid()) {
            throw new ValidationErrorException("Timesheet violates business rules: " + 
                validation.getViolations().stream()
                    .map(RuleViolation::getMessage)
                    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ")));
        }
        
        // Apply any automatic adjustments
        applyAutomaticAdjustments(timesheet, validation);
    }
}
```

## Caching and Performance

### 1. Service-Level Caching
```java
@Service
public class TimesheetService {
    
    @Cacheable(value = "timesheets", key = "#id")
    public TimesheetResponseDto getTimesheetById(Integer id) {
        Timesheet timesheet = timesheetRepository.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("Timesheet not found"));
        return timesheetMapper.toResponseDto(timesheet);
    }
    
    @CacheEvict(value = "timesheets", key = "#id")
    public void deleteTimesheet(Integer id) {
        timesheetRepository.deleteById(id);
    }
    
    @CachePut(value = "timesheets", key = "#id")
    public TimesheetResponseDto updateTimesheet(Integer id, TimesheetRequestDto request) {
        // Update logic
    }
}
```

## Service Composition

### 1. Orchestrating Multiple Services
```java
@Service
public class TimesheetWorkflowService {
    
    private final TimesheetService timesheetService;
    private final TimeLogService timeLogService;
    private final ApprovalService approvalService;
    private final NotificationService notificationService;
    
    @Transactional
    public TimesheetWorkflowResult processTimesheetSubmission(TimesheetSubmissionDto submission) {
        // Create timesheet
        TimesheetResponseDto timesheet = timesheetService.createTimesheet(submission.timesheetRequest());
        
        // Create time logs
        List<TimeLogResponseDto> timeLogs = timeLogService.createTimeLogs(
            submission.timeLogRequests(), timesheet.id());
        
        // Submit for approval
        ApprovalResponseDto approval = approvalService.submitForApproval(timesheet.id());
        
        // Send notifications
        notificationService.sendSubmissionNotification(timesheet, timeLogs);
        
        return new TimesheetWorkflowResult(timesheet, timeLogs, approval);
    }
}
```

## Cognitive Complexity [STRICT — MAX 12]

**RULE**: Every method in the codebase MUST have a cognitive complexity of 12 or less. No exceptions.

Cognitive complexity counts: `if`, `else if`, `else`, `for`, `while`, `catch`, `switch`, ternary operators, `&&`, `||`, nested conditions (with nesting increments), and recursion. Each nesting level adds +1 to the increment for nested structures.

### How to Reduce Cognitive Complexity

1. **Extract validation logic** into dedicated private methods (e.g., `validateReopenAccess(...)`, `validateReopenStatus(...)`)
2. **Extract entity mutation** into private methods (e.g., `resetReimbursementForReopen(...)`, `createStatusHistory(...)`)
3. **Extract complex conditionals** into well-named boolean methods (e.g., `isReopenable(status)`)
4. **Use early returns** to avoid deep nesting
5. **Never inline** multiple validation checks + entity setters + persistence calls in a single method

### Enforcement Examples

**FORBIDDEN — method exceeding complexity 12:**
```java
public ResponseDto process(RequestDto request) {
    // auth checks (2 branches)
    // timesheet validation (1 branch)
    // invoice check (1 branch)
    // entity lookup (1 branch)
    // status check (2 branches via && / ||)
    // 7 setters
    // status history creation with 6 setters
    // mapper call
    // Total cognitive complexity: ~15+
}
```

**REQUIRED — decomposed into focused methods:**
```java
public ResponseDto process(RequestDto request) {
    validateAccess(userTypeId, timesheetId, accountId);
    Entity entity = findEntity(id, timesheetId, accountId);
    validateStatus(entity);
    updateEntity(entity, userId, userTypeId, timestamp);
    Entity saved = repository.save(entity);
    createStatusHistory(saved.getId(), statusId, remark, userId, userTypeId, timestamp, accountId);
    return mapper.toResponseDto(saved, statusLabel);
}
```

### Pre-Commit Validation
- [ ] Every public and private method has cognitive complexity <= 12
- [ ] Validation logic is in separate private methods
- [ ] Entity mutation (setters) is in separate private methods
- [ ] No method does auth + validation + mutation + persistence + mapping all inline

## Best Practices

### 1. Service Design
- Keep services focused on single responsibility
- Use interfaces for testability and flexibility
- Implement proper error handling and logging
- Use transactions appropriately

### 2. Business Logic
- Validate business rules at service level
- Use rule engine for complex business logic
- Implement proper permission checking
- Handle bulk operations efficiently

### 3. Performance
- Use caching for frequently accessed data
- Implement bulk operations for multiple records
- Use read-only transactions for queries
- Optimize database queries

### 4. Testing
- Write unit tests for all business logic
- Mock dependencies for isolated testing
- Test error scenarios and edge cases
- Use integration tests for complex workflows

### 5. Cognitive Complexity (STRICT — enforced by SonarQube quality gate)
> **Every method must have a Cognitive Complexity score strictly below 15.**
> A PR that introduces or leaves a method with CC ≥ 15 must not be merged.

Reduce CC by:
- Using **guard clauses** (early return) instead of wrapping logic in `if` blocks
- **Extracting inner loops** into focused private helper methods — each has its own CC budget
- **Extracting predicate methods** for multi-`&&`/`||` conditions
- **Pre-computing boolean flags** before loops to avoid compound conditions inside them
- Replacing filter-then-transform patterns with **streams** (adds zero CC)

```java
// ❌ PROHIBITED — deeply nested loops push CC above 15
public void process(Parent parent) {
    if (parent.getChildren() != null) {
        for (Child child : parent.getChildren()) {
            if (child.getItems() != null) {
                for (Item item : child.getItems()) {
                    if (item.isActive() && item.getValue() != null) {
                        total += item.getValue();
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

// ✅ REQUIRED — each method independently stays well below 15
public void process(Parent parent) {          // CC = 2
    if (parent.getChildren() == null) return;
    for (Child child : parent.getChildren()) {
        collectFromChild(child);
    }
}

private void collectFromChild(Child child) {  // CC = 3
    if (child.getItems() == null) return;
    for (Item item : child.getItems()) {
        if (isActiveWithValue(item)) total += item.getValue();
    }
}

private boolean isActiveWithValue(Item item) { // CC = 1 (one &&)
    return item.isActive() && item.getValue() != null;
}
```



